{
226: {skill_tested: "Identify a detail that changes the meaning of a record.", question: "In Passage A, what does the replacement of a crossed-out name with Rosa indicate?", option1: "Rosa owned Maple Lanes.", option2: "The league prohibited factory employees from bowling.", option3: "The score sheet belonged to a children's league.", option4: "Rosa substituted when the regular team was delayed at work.", correctAnswer: "Rosa substituted when the regular team was delayed at work.", explanation: "Step 1: The sheet replaces another player's name with Rosa's.<br><br>Step 2: Dad explains that she filled in when machine breakdowns kept the regular team at work.<br><br>Answer: Rosa substituted when the regular team was delayed at work.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
227: {skill_tested: "Identify the climatologist's qualified assessment.", question: "How does the climatologist initially respond to the weather notebooks in Passage B?", option1: "She identifies limitations and asks for contextual evidence that could support careful use.", option2: "She accepts every reading without adjustment.", option3: "She rejects them because they were recorded by a volunteer.", option4: "She treats the margins as more precise than automated stations.", correctAnswer: "She identifies limitations and asks for contextual evidence that could support careful use.", explanation: "Step 1: She notes problems involving time, equipment, and uneven rainfall.<br><br>Step 2: She still asks about equipment changes and nearby stations rather than dismissing the notebooks.<br><br>Answer: She identifies limitations and asks for contextual evidence that could support careful use.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
228: {skill_tested: "Identify a shared central idea.", question: "Which statement best describes an idea shared by both passages?", option1: "A record is useful only if every entry supports one purpose.", option2: "An ordinary record can become valuable when its context and limitations are preserved.", option3: "Conflicting memories should be removed from an archive.", option4: "Numerical records matter chiefly when they document exceptional performance.", correctAnswer: "An ordinary record can become valuable when its context and limitations are preserved.", explanation: "Step 1: Bowling sheets reveal work, family, and participation when annotated.<br><br>Step 2: Weather readings become usable through notes, flags, and local context.<br><br>Answer: An ordinary record can become valuable when its context and limitations are preserved.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
229: {skill_tested: "Analyze how a list broadens the meaning of numerical records.", question: "The factory mergers, children's drawings, and shortened winter games in Passage A primarily show that the score sheets:", option1: "were designed as official factory reports.", option2: "prove that scores steadily declined each year.", option3: "document community circumstances beyond bowling totals.", option4: "contain no useful information about participation.", correctAnswer: "document community circumstances beyond bowling totals.", explanation: "Step 1: Each detail connects a sheet to work, family life, or fuel conditions.<br><br>Step 2: Those details extend the records beyond the scores printed on them.<br><br>Step 3: The list supports the description of the sheets as a calendar of ordinary participation.<br><br>Answer: document community circumstances beyond bowling totals.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
230: {skill_tested: "Infer why metadata affects scientific reuse.", question: "Why is the note about moving the rain gauge important in Passage B?", option1: "It guarantees that every earlier reading was incorrect.", option2: "It allows missing days to be assigned exact rainfall totals.", option3: "It proves the new roof created all later storms.", option4: "It may explain a change in readings caused by the instrument's location rather than the weather.", correctAnswer: "It may explain a change in readings caused by the instrument's location rather than the weather.", explanation: "Step 1: Gauge placement can affect how rain is collected.<br><br>Step 2: A location change can therefore alter measurements even if climate does not change.<br><br>Step 3: Recording the move helps later analysts distinguish instrumental and environmental causes.<br><br>Answer: It may explain a change in readings caused by the instrument's location rather than the weather.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
231: {skill_tested: "Compare selective preservation decisions.", question: "The narrator's selection of bowling sheets and the climatologist's selection of weather readings are similar because both:", option1: "preserve or use a subset according to a stated purpose instead of treating completeness as the only form of value.", option2: "discard all records containing uncertainty.", option3: "choose only the highest numerical values.", option4: "exclude records that community members remember.", correctAnswer: "preserve or use a subset according to a stated purpose instead of treating completeness as the only form of value.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A selects representative sheets and removes unreadable or duplicate material.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B uses only suitable readings for regional analysis while retaining the notebooks.<br><br>Step 3: Both distinguish preserving a record from requiring every part to perform every task.<br><br>Answer: preserve or use a subset according to a stated purpose instead of treating completeness as the only form of value.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
232: {skill_tested: "Analyze a narrator's emotional revision.", question: "The Passage B narrator's response to excluded pages changes when the narrator realizes that:", option1: "scientific analysis is the only reason to keep any notebook.", option2: "a record can retain historical and local value even if some entries do not fit a particular analysis.", option3: "all excluded readings will later be proven exact.", option4: "the firefighters no longer remember using the notebooks.", correctAnswer: "a record can retain historical and local value even if some entries do not fit a particular analysis.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator initially feels protective when only part of the series is used.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator then recognizes that not every entry must serve every purpose.<br><br>Step 3: Scientific exclusion does not erase the notebooks' evidence of discipline and local decisions.<br><br>Answer: a record can retain historical and local value even if some entries do not fit a particular analysis.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
233: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the relationship between routine and historical significance.", question: "Taken together, the passages suggest that routine records often gain historical significance because they:", option1: "were originally created to impress future archivists.", option2: "contain fewer errors than records created for research.", option3: "capture repeated practices and surrounding circumstances that may disappear when the routine ends.", option4: "describe only the most unusual events in a community.", correctAnswer: "capture repeated practices and surrounding circumstances that may disappear when the routine ends.", explanation: "Step 1: Score sheets preserve league participation, work changes, and family presence.<br><br>Step 2: Weather notebooks preserve daily observation and decisions at the fire station.<br><br>Step 3: Their regularity records structures of community life that become visible after those practices change.<br><br>Answer: capture repeated practices and surrounding circumstances that may disappear when the routine ends.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
234: {skill_tested: "Compare how uncertainty contributes to trustworthy preservation.", question: "Why do both narrators retain signs of disagreement or uncertainty in their archives?", option1: "Uncertainty makes every possible interpretation equally accurate.", option2: "Contradictory memories can substitute for damaged physical records without qualification.", option3: "Future readers are expected to ignore all flagged entries.", option4: "Recording evidentiary limits prevents a convenient single account from appearing more certain than the material supports.", correctAnswer: "Recording evidentiary limits prevents a convenient single account from appearing more certain than the material supports.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A catalogs conflicting memories rather than selecting the neatest story.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B flags uncertain times, changed equipment, and missing days.<br><br>Step 3: Those qualifications allow later use while preserving the boundary between evidence and unsupported certainty.<br><br>Answer: Recording evidentiary limits prevents a convenient single account from appearing more certain than the material supports.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
